# Seed data containing real people's personal details.
#
# This repository is PUBLIC. These files carry ~2,200 personal email
# addresses, ~960 phone numbers and ~380 dates of birth belonging to event
# attendees and hackathon participants, none of whom agreed to have them
# published. Committing them once puts them in the git history permanently —
# deleting the file later does not remove them.
#
# The files live in the working copy and are applied to the database directly.
# They must never be tracked here.
supabase/seed/*.sql
supabase/seed/hackathons/*.sql

# Supabase CLI scratch. `functions deploy` writes the linked project ref here;
# it is machine state, not project state.
supabase/.temp/

# Credential files, as insurance rather than as a live risk.
#
# tools/generate-vapid-keys.mjs writes to C:\sctools\vapid.env — deliberately
# OUTSIDE this repository, so nothing here can reach it. These patterns exist
# for the day somebody runs a generator from inside the tree, or drops a .env
# beside a function to test it locally.
#
# This repository is PUBLIC, and a committed private key cannot be un-published
# by deleting it later — it has to be rotated. Rotating the VAPID key
# invalidates every member's push subscription, because browsers bind a
# subscription to the key that created it.
*.env
.env.*
vapid*.txt
vapid*.json

# Dependencies. Nothing in this repo is built or bundled — the site is served
# as plain files — so node_modules only ever appears when somebody installs a
# package to run a script in tools/. It was untracked and UNIGNORED until a
# 7.2MB copy of `xlsx` landed here by accident, one `git add -A` away from
# being committed to a public repository. Run tools that need a package from
# outside the repo, or accept that this line is what stands between you and
# publishing a dependency tree.
node_modules/
